## 7. Blocking findings (round 3)

### B1 — the claimed truncation behaviour is false in the real officina

context.ts:107 calls the section "one line, never dropped by truncation".
It is dropped. §2 above: at --max-tokens 600 the pointer is gone for
eng-lead, qa-lead, builder-a and builder-b — every builder and the censor,
the four sellae the pointer exists for. It survives only for patron,
producer and architect, whose leges are small enough that nothing needs
dropping.

The drop loop (context.ts:198-205) removes the highest priority *number*
first. At priority 2 the pointer is the fourth of five sections dropped,
ahead of the lex. Behaviour 24 passes because the fixture officina's lex is
~200 tokens; the real engineering lex is ~1100, so 600 tokens forces the
loop past the pointer and on to the lex, leaving a two-line output that is
nothing but "truncated: ...".

§4: priority 0 makes the comment true for every sella at 600 with no test
change. One character.

### B2 — round 2's pass conditions are unmet; the gate cannot be re-opened on this tree

§6. The brief is unchanged, the five declared behaviours still have no reds
(opus.red_evidence, blocking), and tests/lint/types still certify
tree:5ab03a0b while the PR head tree is tree:d34e898e — three
probatio.certifies.stale advisories on this opus's own front matter.

D-015 is explicit that this is the point of rebasing: "the gates must
re-run before the PR can pass, because the evidence model already refuses
stale certificates." They were not re-run. The PR's test plan asserts "npm
test — 0 failures on the rebased tree"; that is true (I re-ran it) but no
certificate records it, and a claim in a PR body is not a probatio.

### B3 — the work is outside the opus's declared files

briefs/W-028.md owns three files: rules/process.ts, rules/process.test.ts,
briefs/W-028.md. This PR touches six source files, none of them declared.
Behaviours 22-24 are not an overflow of the brief's numbering — they are a
different subject from all five of its behaviours, which concern
checkpointStale. Numbering past 5 was the wrong call: it treats a brief the
opus has outgrown as a numbering offset rather than a document to amend,
and it makes opus.red_evidence permanently unsatisfiable, because reds for
1-5 can only be produced against behaviours the brief still describes. This
is round 2's B3 unchanged, now with a third deliverable stacked on it.

The engineering lex's "one opus owns its declared files" and the QA lex's
"(check: probatio.declared)" both cover this; probatio.declared is still
not implemented, so nothing caught it mechanically.

### B4 — the PR promotes a reference that is already wrong

The deliverable tells every agent the banner is the authority on flag
shapes. §5: at PR head the banner omits `[--cwd <dir>]` from `red` and the
`context` subcommand from `hook-event`, both of which the code accepts and
both of which the commands' own usage constants document. The drift is
pre-existing, but this PR is what converts it from a stale string into an
instruction every boot repeats.

Moving USAGE from cli to commands single-sources nothing that was ever
duplicated — there was one copy before and one copy after. The duplication
that matters is banner vs. 33 per-command *_USAGE constants vs. the
parseFlags allowlists, and it is live today. One test asserting every
per-command usage line appears verbatim in USAGE would close the class and
is smaller than the two string fixes it would force.

## 8. Advisory findings

A1. The PR body misdescribes red 22. It says the mutation was "removing the
    pointer"; §3 shows that mutation fails behaviours 22 and 24 together,
    while 22.log records 24 passing. The red is genuine, distinct and
    assertion-level — it just proves the sentence is asserted, not that the
    section is. Reds 23 and 24 reproduce exactly as described. An
    inaccurate account of what was mutated is the cheapest kind of evidence
    defect to fix and the most expensive to discover later.

A2. All three reds carry "# sella: guest". `bisellium red --sella <id>` was
    available; the evidence is unattributable as recorded.

A3. context.test.ts:73-75 asserts on the literal "bisellium check [dir]"
    rather than deriving it from USAGE — the one restatement of banner text
    this PR adds, in the file whose subject is not restating banner text.
    `USAGE.split("\n")[0]` costs nothing and cannot drift.

A4. Mutation M6 survived: the pointer sentence can be cut to "Run
    `bisellium` with no arguments. " and all three behaviours pass. The
    warning that gives the sentence its force — strict allowlists, a wrong
    invocation writing real bookkeeping — is untested. This is the
    "asserted that it fired, not what it did" pattern; milder here than in
    the three opera that failed round 2 for it, because the imperative
    opening is pinned.

A5. Mutation M5 survived: priority 2 -> 3 is not caught. Once B1 is fixed
    the behaviour worth pinning is "present at the tightest budget that
    renders anything for the largest real lex", not "present at 600 in the
    fixture".

## 9. Not a defect

The pointer-over-inline trade is right, and by a wider margin than the PR
claims. Measured: USAGE is 2833 chars = 709 tokens, 17.7% of the 4000-token
default. The pointer is 266 chars = 67 tokens, 1.7%. A 10x saving on a
reference most boots never need, in exchange for one round-trip an agent
about to make a CLI call is already positioned to make. Inlining would have
been wrong.

The cycle reasoning is correct and I verified it rather than took it:
commands declares no dependency on cli, cli declares commands. The shim
follows the W-016 pattern exactly. All four of main.ts's usage-error paths
still print the banner.

## 10. Verdict

FAIL — round 3.

The feature is the right feature and the design judgement inside it is
sound. What fails is everything around it: a behaviour claimed and
commented that the code does not deliver for the sellae it was built for
(B1), round 2's three conditions carried over untouched (B2), a third
undeclared deliverable stacked on a brief that stopped describing this opus
two deliverables ago (B3), and an instruction to trust a reference that is
wrong in two places (B4).

To pass:
  - priority 0 on the cli usage section, and a behaviour that pins survival
    against a lex the size of the real one;
  - amend briefs/W-028.md — or, better, split the usage-banner work into
    its own opus with its own brief, since it shares nothing with
    "patron-as-agent" but a number;
  - reds for every numbered behaviour the amended brief declares;
  - re-record tests/lint/types against the tree that merges;
  - fix the two banner omissions and add the test that keeps them fixed;
  - correct the PR body's account of red 22.

B4 alone would be an advisory. Combined with B1-B3 it is the same finding
the officina has now made three times in a row on this opus: the code is
fine and the evidence does not say what it claims to say.
